WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.060 --> 00:00:04.430
Film Courage: How important was it for you
to make a feature film before you turned thirty?

00:00:04.430 --> 00:00:08.520
Chris von Hoffmann, horror writer/director:
It was very important.

00:00:08.520 --> 00:00:20.679
I’ve been wanting to make a feature at 19
I was trying to make a feature, at 22-23 I

00:00:20.679 --> 00:00:26.870
was trying to make a feature (they were all
different movies) and then at 24 I was trying

00:00:26.870 --> 00:00:28.150
to make another feature.

00:00:28.150 --> 00:00:35.230
At 25 I spent a year trying (that one got
the furthest).

00:00:35.230 --> 00:00:41.050
And we had a sizzle reel that we shot, a pitch
package, we had a business, we had a script,

00:00:41.050 --> 00:00:44.100
we had a lot of stuff packaged for that movie.

00:00:44.100 --> 00:00:50.540
But we would have people come on, they would
fall off, we just couldn’t get…it was

00:00:50.540 --> 00:00:54.470
like a $200,000 contract…it probably need
like $200,000, it was like 25 locations (a

00:00:54.470 --> 00:00:56.450
lot of them were interior), it was another
desert movie.

00:00:56.450 --> 00:00:59.711
And I think I took a lot of that stuff and
put it inside of DRIFTER, like a lot of the

00:00:59.711 --> 00:01:04.979
thematic of that movie and just made DRIFTER
a little bit more cheaper and a little bit

00:01:04.979 --> 00:01:06.270
more accessible.

00:01:06.270 --> 00:01:08.340
I spent a year on that movie throughout 2013.

00:01:08.340 --> 00:01:13.399
Some people responded to the script, some
people didn’t.

00:01:13.399 --> 00:01:16.770
Some people just didn’t get it.

00:01:16.770 --> 00:01:24.850
Because it wasn’t very plot heavy, it was
a very character driven story, this really

00:01:24.850 --> 00:01:29.579
kind of like whacked out cross-country story,
sort of like it was from California to Texas.

00:01:29.579 --> 00:01:40.630
And 
at a certain point I was like “I’ve got

00:01:40.630 --> 00:01:42.061
to drop this on.”

00:01:42.061 --> 00:01:46.770
And I made a couple shorts out of frustration
to be creatively stimulated.

00:01:46.770 --> 00:01:52.079
And then I was like 27 when I decided to just
jump into it with DRIFTER and I was like just

00:01:52.079 --> 00:01:54.009
set the dates, I got locked in dates.

00:01:54.009 --> 00:01:58.130
I was just like I’m going to bang this thing
out.

00:01:58.130 --> 00:02:04.740
At least I can have a movie with a feature
film run time, I can at least have a feature

00:02:04.740 --> 00:02:05.770
under my belt.

00:02:05.770 --> 00:02:09.670
It might not be amazing but I have a feature
technically.

00:02:09.670 --> 00:02:14.810
So that was just very important to me to make
a feature film as soon as possible.

00:02:14.810 --> 00:02:20.700
But it’s like there is aways someone out
there that made that feature before you.

00:02:20.700 --> 00:02:28.530
Soderbergh was 25 or 26, Scorsese 26, there’s
always someone out there to beat you to the

00:02:28.530 --> 00:02:29.530
punch.

00:02:29.530 --> 00:02:35.129
Because I spent a lot of time acting so I
was focusing on that for awhile.

00:02:35.129 --> 00:02:41.290
Yes, it was very, very important for me to
make a feature before I was 30, very important

00:02:41.290 --> 00:02:42.290
to me.

00:02:42.290 --> 00:02:44.519
Film Courage: Did you crowdfund for DRIFTER?

00:02:44.519 --> 00:02:49.079
Chris: Um, yeah…we tried.

00:02:49.079 --> 00:02:51.710
It just wasn’t successful at all.

00:02:51.710 --> 00:02:55.000
It was like embarrassedly unsuccessful.

00:02:55.000 --> 00:02:59.820
I realize that it just wasn’t the kind of
movie that…I mean I’ll never do that kind

00:02:59.820 --> 00:03:00.820
of stuff again.

00:03:00.820 --> 00:03:03.090
I really kind of feel better for ever really
starting that campaign.

00:03:03.090 --> 00:03:07.230
I didn’t feel like that movie leant itself
to that kind of campaign very well because

00:03:07.230 --> 00:03:15.871
all I was trying to make was a really psychological
disturbing cannibal movie and we didn’t

00:03:15.871 --> 00:03:18.370
have any hook.

00:03:18.370 --> 00:03:22.599
Some of the people were familiar with a couple
shorts that I made, we didn’t have any hook.

00:03:22.599 --> 00:03:23.599
We didn’t any hook.

00:03:23.599 --> 00:03:28.820
We didn’t have any big genre actor attached,
it wasn’t based on a field game or a graphic

00:03:28.820 --> 00:03:30.930
novel or anything.

00:03:30.930 --> 00:03:42.819
It was just what it was and there was just
no hook to it.

00:03:42.819 --> 00:03:49.420
There were some nice side donators but a lot
of it was personal sacrifice.

00:03:49.420 --> 00:03:57.040
I mean I don’t know if I recommend that
to a lot of filmmakers to do that and a lot

00:03:57.040 --> 00:04:04.849
of filmmakers have a lot of success crowdfunding,
and I just don’t even know how they do that?

00:04:04.849 --> 00:04:10.019
I never understood the mystery of Indiegogo
and Kickstarter, I just don’t get it.

00:04:10.019 --> 00:04:14.760
I don’t understand how they get this money,
I don’t.

00:04:14.760 --> 00:04:19.500
So I’ve never had any success with any crowdfunding
campaigns.

00:04:19.500 --> 00:04:23.780
I’ve tried a couple including that one and
I’ve just never had any success with it.

00:04:23.780 --> 00:04:32.250
It’s like I’m not going to just like drop
this.

00:04:32.250 --> 00:04:35.320
Especially at that time I needed to make a
feature.

00:04:35.320 --> 00:04:45.030
And I just lived really cheaply, lived really
uncomfortably so I could save money and just

00:04:45.030 --> 00:04:50.080
put it all towards the movie.

